---
// firma2-project-comments — the notes ON this record: the running account of
// coordination around a project that a program manager keeps beside the numbers
// instead of in an inbox. The last section of the project's main column.
//
// WHY THE MAIN COLUMN, AND WHY LAST (user, 2026-10-22). The record page sorts
// its content by Linear's test — things you READ go in the main column, things
// you SET go in the rail — and a conversation is read. It spent two releases as
// the third tab of the rail, one click from the facts, on the argument that a
// comment is the reason a number looks the way it does; that argument was for
// PROXIMITY, and a tab hid the thread entirely until it was chosen. Linear
// splits conversation in two: project updates live in the main column and the
// property change log lives in the sidebar as Activity. These comments are
// discussion, closer to updates than to a log — the History sheet already plays
// the Activity role — so they close the document the way an activity feed
// closes an issue: after everything the thread is about.
//
// THE COUNT RIDES IN THE HEADING, above zero only. On the tab it was a badge,
// because a tab whose content might be empty needs to say so from outside. In
// the main column the section is always on screen, so the number is the card's
// subtitle — the lego's slot for "a short datum qualifying the title" — and the
// empty state says the rest for a thread with nothing in it. No "0": the empty
// state is already the zero.
//
// NEWEST FIRST. A comment thread is read to find out WHAT CHANGED, not to
// reconstruct a history, so the most recent note is the one that gets the top
// of the card. This inverts the milestone timeline above it, which runs
// oldest-first — deliberately: a timeline is a shape you read along, a thread is
// a stack you read the top of.
//
// AUTHOR AND DATE SHARE A LINE, pushed apart. They are the two things a reader
// scans a thread by ("who said it" / "how stale is it") and neither is the
// note, so they ride above it on one row. The `.repel` that arranges them
// belongs to firma2-contact-card (the date goes in its `trailing` slot) rather
// than to this file — see the note at the call site for why the card, not the
// thread, has to own that row.
//
// AND THE AUTHOR IS A CONTACT CARD, not a name. Every commenter is one of the
// record's own contacts, so the thread renders the same firma2-contact-card the
// Contacts section renders: hover or focus a name and you get their role, their
// organization and their address without the thread having to print any of it.
//
// NO COMPOSER. Linear's project overview opens with a "write the first update"
// card; this is a prototype with no write path for comments, and a composer
// would be a promise the page cannot keep. The inert Add comment button says
// the same thing honestly (brief §4, "Inert, not disabled").
//
// AN EMPTY THREAD IS A REAL STATE, and a quarter of the portfolio is in it (the
// mock derives the count from the project's own name — see
// firma2-project-detail). That is on purpose: an empty state nobody can reach
// is an empty state nobody reviews. It says what is absent and nothing else —
// the recovery action is the button in the card's header, so the empty state
// does not also describe it.
//
// bcn-lego-checked: no esa- comment/thread/activity/feed lego — walked the
// catalog (`ls node_modules/@esa/ecology/src/components/`): the closest are
// esa-alert-box (a message ABOUT the page, addressed to the reader, not a
// record of who said what) and esa-card, which IS the section's surface here.
// Beacon is not cloned on this machine. Everything else is a lego or a
// component: firma2-contact-card is the author, esa-empty-state is the empty
// thread, esa-button is the Add affordance, and the layout is `.stack` with no
// bespoke flex anywhere.
import EsaCard from '@esa/ecology/esa-card.astro';
import Firma2ContactCard from '../shared/firma2-contact-card.astro';
import EsaEmptyState from '@esa/ecology/esa-empty-state.astro';
import EsaButton from '@esa/ecology/esa-button.astro';
import type { ProjectComment } from '../../data/firma2-project-detail';

interface Props {
  /** Newest first; may be empty. */
  comments: ProjectComment[];
}

const { comments } = Astro.props;
const count = comments.length;
---
<EsaCard title="Comments" subtitle={count > 0 ? String(count) : undefined}>
  <!-- ALWAYS AVAILABLE, like Record work: adding a note is not an edit of the
       record's fields, so it does not wait for Edit mode. Inert, and
       deliberately NOT `disabled` — the same stance every Add affordance on
       this page takes. "Add comment" rather than "Comment": the verb form
       matches its siblings (Add measure, Add source, Add milestone). -->
  <EsaButton slot="actions" variant="chrome" size="sm" icon="plus">Add comment</EsaButton>

  {count === 0 ? (
    <!-- No description — the Add button in the header IS the recovery action,
         and an empty state that also spells it out is the same instruction
         twice. -->
    <EsaEmptyState size="sm" title="No comments yet" />
  ) : (
    <ol class="firma2-project-comments stack" data-gap="lg">
      {comments.map((comment) => (
        <li class="stack" data-gap="2xs">
          {/* THE AUTHOR IS THE SAME OBJECT THE CONTACTS SECTION RENDERS — the
              whole reason firma2-contact-card is a shared component and the
              reason a comment carries its author as a CONTACT rather than as a
              copied name. It also deletes a line from every note: the
              commenter's organization used to print under their name here, and
              it now lives in the card, one hover away.

              THE DATE RIDES IN THE CARD'S `trailing` SLOT rather than beside
              it in a `.repel` of this file's own. The card's trigger has to
              span the row — esa-popover centres its panel on the trigger — and
              two things cannot both span one row. The card repels its own
              byline instead, which lands the date in the same place with none
              of the geometry split across two files. */}
          <Firma2ContactCard contact={comment.author} nameClass="typography-label-sm-strong">
            {/* A <time> would be the right element and this mock has no
                machine date to put in its datetime — the record stores the
                rendered string, for the determinism reason firma2-project-
                detail states. A <time> with no datetime is just a span that
                claims to be more, so it is a span. */}
            <span slot="trailing" class="firma2-project-comments__date typography-body-xs">
              {comment.date}
            </span>
          </Firma2ContactCard>
          <p class="firma2-project-comments__body typography-body-sm">{comment.body}</p>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ol>
  )}
</EsaCard>

<style>
  /* An <ol> in a card: the ordinals carry nothing a reader wants (a comment is
     identified by who and when, not by its number) but the ORDER is real, so
     the semantics stay and only the marker goes. layouts.css zeroes the UA
     MARGIN on ul/ol at :where() but not the 40px marker gutter, so the padding
     has to go explicitly or the thread sits two thirds of an inch right of the
     card's own edge. */
  .firma2-project-comments {
    list-style: none;
    padding-inline-start: 0;
  }

  .firma2-project-comments__date {
    color: var(--color-content-default-secondary, #646464);
    margin: 0;
    /* The date must not wrap mid-string when the author's name is long — `.repel`
       wraps the PAIR, which is the behaviour we want, and this keeps the date
       whole when it does. */
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  .firma2-project-comments__body {
    margin: 0;
    /* Primary ink: the note is the content of this card, and the two lines
       above it are its metadata. Reversing that — a grey note under a black
       name — is the read every comment thread that looks like a changelog got
       wrong. */
    color: var(--color-content-default, #202020);
  }
</style>
